In this lesson we'll look at how to solve angle problems and give proper reasons. The hard part isn't the arithmetic, it's picking the right pair of angles and saying why they relate. So here's your recipe. First, draw the diagram or picture it in your head, and mark any angles you already know. Next, look at the unknown angle and figure out which type of pair it forms with a known angle. Is it corresponding, alternate, or co-interior? Then write the reason clearly, like "equal, corresponding angles, parallel lines" or "supplementary, co-interior angles, parallel lines." After that, write the equation and solve it. Now here's a key idea. Once you've got parallel lines in play, you also unlock some other relationships. Vertically opposite angles are always equal, even without parallel lines. Angles on a straight line add to one hundred and eighty degrees, and angles around a point add to three hundred and sixty. These work in any diagram. A quick warning before we go. In a strict marking scheme, writing "they are equal" without naming the angle type loses marks, so always attach the reason. Now let's see it in action.